  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Mon Jun 15 2026\n\nThe Great Lakes remain under cool NW flow with broad troughing over \nthe northern quarter of the US. Early this morning, water vapor and \nRAP analysis indicate an embedded shortwave near IA, but this \nremains well to our south with mostly clear skies over the UP. \nTemperatures are falling back quite nicely, already in the lower to \nmid 40s across much of the area. I would not be shocked to see some \nof the usual suspects falling into the upper 30s. \n\nHeading into the daytime hours, quiet, sunny, and dry conditions \ngive way to increasing clouds and eventually chances for showers \ninto the afternoon and evening as another shortwave drops into the \nNorthern Plains and heads towards the Great Lakes. Combined forcing \nfrom this approaching wave and building diurnal instability may \nsupport the development of afternoon showers/thunderstorms, \nparticularly across the south-central and east half. That said, \ncoverage of showers and storms increases this evening as the trough \naxis finally shifts into the area, tapering off after 06Z. We are \ncertainly not looking at a washout, with most spots coming in below \na tenth of an inch, but guidance does show around a 25% chance for \nsome higher totals in excess of a quarter inch where any heavier \ndownpours can occur. Otherwise, expect temperatures to come in \nmilder compared to Sunday, peaking in the lower 70s for most, and in \nthe upper 60s onshore of Lake Michigan. Temperatures fall back into \nthe mid 40s to lower 50s overnight. \n\nAdditional shower/thunderstorm development is expected Tuesday as \nthe midlevel shortwave reflects a weak surface low through Lower \nMichigan. Deterministic guidance then hones in on a stronger \nshortwave and upper level jet streak descending out of the Canadian \nRockies into the Midwest Wednesday through Thursday. The main \nforcing for precipitation remains south of the UP, thus ensemble \nprobability for rainfall amounts >0.5\" by Thursday morning remain \nstalled between 40-60%, greatest across the southern half of the UP. \nBroad troughing is expected to continue over central/eastern Canada \ngoing into the next weekend, suggesting daily rain/thunderstorm \nchances should be expected with continued below normal temperatures.",
